cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use cielo-bench, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "cielo-bench: Check or agree? A benchmark of whether tool-using AI agents verify a user's claim or agree with it"
abstract: >-
  cielo-bench measures sycophancy in tool-using AI agents against verifiable ground truth.
  Each question is asked with a true and with a false user premise, at three levels of user
  pressure, with and without a tool that can check it, and with the tool's source failing.
  Answers are scored deterministically, without an LLM judge. The headline metric,
  agree-after-check, is the share of false-premise trials in which the agent still agrees
  with the user after its own tool returned contrary evidence. Domains: astronomy (NASA/JPL
  Horizons ephemerides) and tabular company data (a synthetic SQLite database queried with
  read-only SQL). An MCP server exposes the same tools.
